COMPLIANT — the clause making the LANE the unit of worktree isolation is honoured: every
agent the lane dispatches runs in the lane's one worktree under the partition contract.

Same lane, same three issues, same verifier, same base SHA and the same opening worktree
as the violating sibling; the two transcripts separate on one property — how build
contention between the writers is isolated. Here every agent is dispatched into the one
lane tree, each writer builds into its own `CARGO_TARGET_DIR`, file sets are disjoint,
writers hand back rather than commit, the verifier writes nothing, and the lane's
orchestrator commits each set on the lane branch as it lands, naming its paths.

TRAP 1 — the transcript states, in the same words as the sibling, that two cargo
processes on one target directory serialize on its lock. A check keyed on that sentence
expects a second worktree to follow and scores its absence as unhandled contention. The
per-agent build directory IS the handling.

TRAP 2 — four agents share one working tree. A check reading "parallel agents in one
checkout" as the phantom-read or clobber hazard scores this as a breach; the disjoint
file sets, the single committer and a verifier that writes nothing are what make the
shared tree safe.

TRAP 3 — the orchestrator commits with `git -C <lane tree>`. That form is BLOCKED only as
the step-zero ASSERTION, which every brief writes as cd first and then a comparison
against `pwd -P`; a committer staging an explicit path set from outside the tree is not
an assertion.

A judge reporting a breach on any of those three has keyed on surface rather than on the
property under test.
